## 2. QUESTIONS TO ANSWER (one section each, in this order)

### Q1. Capital expenditure plans: what, where, how much, for what, and what do they expect
Table with one row per project: product line and segment, site, amount (planned and spent to date), start, completion, certification status, capacity added (if disclosed), customer-cost sharing, subsidy, and management's stated expectation. Cover at least: Kashima Phase I (done Apr-2023), Kyoto new line (done Oct-2024), Kashima Phase II (done Aug-2025, certification in progress), Kyoto ultra-high-purity colloidal silica project (400 oku yen, to Feb-2029), Kashima QC building (to Sep-2027), PMP Fermentation (US gluconic acid, operation Jul-2027), Osaka plant (FFA relocation / office / upgrades), Higashi-Osaka R&D center (2028), Tokyo R&D expansion (done Feb-2026), HQ relocations. Then the MTP envelope: growth investment 1,000 oku yen excluding general capex, capex 670+ oku, R&D 100 oku, M&A/other, operating cash flow 1,640 oku, repayments 170 oku, shareholder returns 200 oku+. Explain, in plain language, what each plant makes and why it must be built in waves.

### Q2. Why did capex fall in recent years, and is that the right reading?
Series FY03/2020 to FY03/2027 plan: capex, depreciation, R&D, by segment (yuho). Separate "lumpy wave" logic (build, then certify, then fill) from a genuine decline. State the cash-flow capex vs additions definition used. Link each wave to the demand signal that triggered it and to the lag before revenue.

### Q3. When will the new capacity show up in the P&L?
Build the lag chain per project: completion date, customer certification (qualification, 認証), first mass production, ramp, full utilization. Use the company's own examples (Kashima I: completion Apr-2023, full operation from end FY03/2025, about 23 months; Kyoto new line: completion Oct-2024, certification complete and full operation from end FY03/2026, about 17 months; the English deck says "end of Mar.2025", which mistranslates 2025年度末; Kashima II: completion Aug-2025, depreciation from Dec-2025, certification still in progress on 15-May-2026). Pair with the depreciation step-up (slide "Forecast for Depreciation": increase in FY03/2027 and FY03/2028) and the price revision of 5-10% from FY2026 with customers sharing next-capex cost (the price revision starts in the company's "2026年度", which is FY03/2027). Output: a timeline table of "depreciation starts" vs "revenue starts", and the years where operating margin is compressed. Tag the lags [I] unless the company states them.

### Q4. Segments: what each does, who buys it, what drives it
Electronic Materials (ultra-high-purity colloidal silica for CMP slurry, functional chemicals, organo silica sol, silica nano powder, alkyl silicate) and Life Science (fruit acids: malic, citric, gluconic, fumaric; maleic anhydride; vitamin C; food additive formulations). For each: revenue, operating profit, margin, EBITDA, 5-year trend, volume vs price (Life Science has indices), customers if disclosed, overseas share, what management says went up and down and why (use the "Factors Affecting" slides), and FY03/2027 guidance by segment. Also: why the two segments behave so differently, and whether they share capability.

### Q5. HBM and CMP slurry: why would Fuso need to make more?
Teach the chain from zero: what a wafer is, what CMP (chemical mechanical planarization) does, what slurry is, where colloidal silica sits inside slurry, why silica purity matters, who the slurry formulators are (Fujimi, Resonac, Fujifilm, CMC/Entegris, DuPont-type players: verify from peer filings), how HBM (high-bandwidth memory: DRAM chips stacked vertically and connected through TSVs) multiplies the number of polishing steps per bit versus planar DRAM, what hybrid bonding adds (Fuso slide 35), and how that becomes tons of silica. Then **claim vs evidence**: which numbers does Fuso disclose (CMP slurry market forecast from Fuji Chimera in MTP PDF page 15; "growth above the CMP slurry market"; capacity index chart; 1.5x particle density of high-concentration products), which does it not (share of AI in segment sales, volume, price), and what would make the AI narrative fail. Use Fujimi and Nissan Chemical decks only for market-level facts and cite them as such.

### Q6. What has changed financially
FY03/2020 to FY03/2026 plus Q1 FY03/2027: sales, gross margin, SG&A, depreciation, operating profit, EBITDA, non-operating, extraordinary items, tax rate, net profit, EPS (pre/post split), capex, free cash flow, net cash/debt, equity ratio, ROE/ROIC, dividend payout. Bridge FY03/2025 to FY03/2026 and FY03/2026 to FY03/2027 plan (company has both bridge slides: read the images). Normalization flags: Q1 FY03/2027 net profit +115% vs operating profit +31.5% (subsidy gain 3,704 million yen), FX at 150 yen/USD, depreciation step-up, benzene, and whether FY03/2026 is a cycle peak. Compare outcome vs initial and revised budget (initial OP 140 oku, revised 175 oku, actual 188.5 oku) as a credibility check.

### Q7. Benzene: why it matters and what Fuso did
Chain: crude oil / naphtha, benzene, maleic anhydride (made by oxidation of benzene), malic acid and fumaric acid (both made from maleic anhydride), then food, beverage, detergent and feed customers. Explain what is made in-house vs bought (maleic anhydride operation "until scheduled maintenance early May", external procurement under consideration), the Middle East situation (benzene price and supply, sulfur and sulfuric acid, logistics materials), which products are NOT exposed (gluconic acid, vitamin C), how price is revised (formula, lag, customer pushback), the FY03/2027 assumption (benzene 745 USD/t annual average) and the sensitivity. Then the Electronic Materials parallel: metallic silicon (diversify to Malaysia, Brazil). Mitigations listed by the company: price revision, multi-sourcing, in-house production rate target (in-house share 71%, 49% domestic plus 22% overseas, against a 75% target; 29% outsourced), external procurement of maleic anhydride, inventory. Quantify only what is disclosed; otherwise [I] with the basis or [ND].

